#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027f07cc-cf92-57e1-a7dd-52d7f06d98a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269292} {:answer/id #uuid "28cefc3d-d321-5b03-a62f-d8e2472c48f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb96c78-c8a0-51e1-a8a5-0e898bc94a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269696} {:answer/id #uuid "88d6f270-4f72-5d1a-bb5b-8fbb6eb96434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270449} {:answer/id #uuid "b73d03e8-8fba-5412-a8cc-88b5936c079c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cefc3d-d321-5b03-a62f-d8e2472c48f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636} :answer-field/id #uuid "a051e209-09e1-5e9e-b803-9d96c9cf127a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33a5a8c7-40f0-5353-a590-88087e77753a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141086" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{-x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(3{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne 3{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=3{e}^{-x}$ and ${y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-x}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-3y \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-3(3{e}^{-x}) \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\ne -9{e}^{-x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=3{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496e15c7-53c7-579c-8873-1e0eda230e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269937} {:answer/id #uuid "5806fb9e-afea-5d8f-9ce2-c668e570e705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270046} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3be53d-e686-5999-8cae-d77e8bfdbcc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270914} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf24249-8330-5344-9a68-ed06fb6b088d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271194} {:answer/id #uuid "dee82fb9-fbfd-5840-bc3a-4c4b47cb6275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496e15c7-53c7-579c-8873-1e0eda230e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269937} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1cdc1fa-0584-5f35-a9ea-2a774691d9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c8904e1-5f17-5951-8dbc-61b76cd0babc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141088" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da8ca83-9810-5e03-afc4-c10c787af01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269514} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdd200c-7408-5659-8fe2-7f44e245f0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270653} {:answer/id #uuid "a2061503-cef9-50bf-a85a-ef2c605b3879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270708} {:answer/id #uuid "e813d0ad-d2f2-5d00-b765-d4005a112175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271297} {:answer/id #uuid "eb031019-568e-5c95-9847-afd852126e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cdd200c-7408-5659-8fe2-7f44e245f0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270653} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f29670-922a-5956-81fd-e1a1924ed0c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8806c061-892c-5fd2-baf1-cd903aa5ea31" :question/math-academy-id "q-141089" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c381dc-376d-5353-8ba0-92a441ad5120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269593} {:answer/id #uuid "5934a80e-f359-52ec-958b-93307b322bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270058} {:answer/id #uuid "8b75f1c9-bcf0-515e-be7b-9d4e518d6c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270477} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bfbf77-64d8-59e2-8500-31531556a0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271019} {:answer/id #uuid "f61137e8-90ef-5d1f-835b-05645581b298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c381dc-376d-5353-8ba0-92a441ad5120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269593} :answer-field/id #uuid "e272136a-ebf1-5cd3-8386-04e9861680b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e106766c-2780-56ea-8754-563b11d222dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-141091" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1$\n- $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 0 & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "174e0d49-7a0b-59e7-b52f-4bbee3f180a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269462} {:answer/id #uuid "366949c8-88b6-5e13-a572-ef57d6d34106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269763} {:answer/id #uuid "97c9d4a8-02fa-5f94-a559-9130ec355a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270605} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f1d177-eeff-524d-a7d5-855d6a8dde3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270878} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0cd67-e050-5916-a080-d88d385d894a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4f1d177-eeff-524d-a7d5-855d6a8dde3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270878} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e625c3e-5398-5433-bca4-04361125f29a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6e3bd06-ef25-5819-bfee-624e8862d278" :question/math-academy-id "q-141092" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22bc4b9d-c71a-59dc-ae37-22fae65e1789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269570} {:answer/id #uuid "9c06b791-ad38-5ac6-b6dc-b9a0da4ba7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270644} {:answer/id #uuid "b8db0ed2-b44d-5a73-a4a9-8538350f5fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270903} {:answer/id #uuid "be07e7e3-322f-5d89-89ed-7461cab98f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270946} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ea9313-1523-5c68-b542-c2a59c465e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be07e7e3-322f-5d89-89ed-7461cab98f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270946} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f0be9a-434e-5230-83a3-aca01a100695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c4f3444-d6eb-515b-bbe6-1939e7e4a2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-141093" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 1 & =2\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-1/2})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{-1/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ -1 & =2x\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181fbe61-da24-5d11-89bb-e15dc750df38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269469} {:answer/id #uuid "82aaf05c-2358-5980-b724-9f112407d897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270398} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd83e6f-18a3-5a3a-839c-70e676840414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270930} {:answer/id #uuid "cda477c4-b885-5340-a037-f0135038c2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271071} {:answer/id #uuid "fc749f5e-12ad-5f00-9fd1-76a11aa02cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcd83e6f-18a3-5a3a-839c-70e676840414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270930} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab5b4d1-b93f-5fb2-8b74-1ed5e5ed886e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbaad5c7-c732-5938-9213-ace2aa1f23df" :question/math-academy-id "q-141098" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=4x$\n- $y=2{x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=4x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(4x)}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\ne 16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y=4x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2{x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(2{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}8x \\\\ -4x & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2{x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\ne 4{x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & \\ne {x}^{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32636bf7-fcdf-57f1-b4f1-80d5f65e2bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269729} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc336a7-6b7e-5387-99f0-8a5cfa4145a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269814} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff4bfb8-9609-52c7-9127-adb44ed125c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269990} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8aeea4-c9b2-5447-8118-eea04ad0ab38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270243} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c5393d-d3ca-5f85-9adf-30ce831e8ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bc336a7-6b7e-5387-99f0-8a5cfa4145a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269814} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a84b979-6b6b-5928-97ca-fd9627439d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b4e4e14-2aab-5c49-ba69-c1255e3d6145" :question/math-academy-id "q-141099" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28284742-46e2-5bfa-8b1f-6416a4816637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269626} {:answer/id #uuid "462e6347-3fb0-5a17-8d03-b15563e977e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269911} {:answer/id #uuid "65a9e267-0a2f-5b55-8861-0445518b56cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270174} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfc6f3e-6a58-515d-a493-cab033a2d4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270234} {:answer/id #uuid "91c725a6-0336-584a-a9e3-828acb6cb769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a9e267-0a2f-5b55-8861-0445518b56cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270174} :answer-field/id #uuid "9becf189-0060-57b9-9574-1a0ea3d595a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1646b369-92a0-554a-b444-f79313d419b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141101" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{5}$\n- $y={x}^{10}$\n- $y=5{x}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{5},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{5})}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & =5{x}^{4}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{5}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{10},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=10{x}^{9}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{10})}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}5{x}^{9} \\\\ 5{x}^{9} & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{10}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=5{x}^{4},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{3}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5(5{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}25{x}^{3} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 5{x}^{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=5{x}^{4}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9b4355-c65b-5ee8-aafc-70cf83ebfa03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269694} {:answer/id #uuid "5da76957-359f-522c-a8e9-62617e8270e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270101} {:answer/id #uuid "6a596190-3114-56c4-87f6-43ef41dd4709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270212} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2b2a49-b8d4-50f2-bcdc-1376f690baec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270350} {:answer/id #uuid "8af21f43-d8c7-5bbf-8e55-9740b508cd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a2b2a49-b8d4-50f2-bcdc-1376f690baec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270350} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4f2def8-f001-5ca5-8e82-5b38b9e502c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8ec718b-9c17-5ad2-8af8-895d469a7090" :question/math-academy-id "q-141103" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043c68c8-e391-5780-a3ff-6d80dc52b081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290656} {:answer/id #uuid "931ab8d4-be2c-5e4d-92d6-fc6433eec3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291265} {:answer/id #uuid "9767e111-d1e9-5924-9dc2-6f58d60a1d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291290} {:answer/id #uuid "bb01b60e-6bb3-5d81-89d4-9256edcd3ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291443} {:answer/id #uuid "d869766d-8d2f-5348-b322-4dd0c0dc459d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043c68c8-e391-5780-a3ff-6d80dc52b081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290656} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15b4588-e9b5-5b8e-af3b-02394e3239c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69137884-05b3-53be-9c7c-95b53b1ed329" :question/math-academy-id "q-27009" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x?$ \n\nI. The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nII. The function has a minimum value at  $x=2\\pi .$\n\nIII. The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c77bec4ebb04668fbff5176418ed9c09143a8660879fdf523a158c5f109006c.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ the function intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a maximum at $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84e0a734-5caa-53e6-95cb-8ebb089583f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295980} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dbaf5a-ef2e-5a0c-af17-283db49b71b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296204} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f54506-f8f9-5e84-a336-30e0e1061dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296499} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf5e982-270e-5ac6-907b-4a07fbb8da8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296515} {:answer/id #uuid "ed52198b-4523-5c6a-85f8-e3abbb92970e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1dbaf5a-ef2e-5a0c-af17-283db49b71b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296204} :answer-field/id #uuid "59ae0b34-6270-5c87-9457-d86beb87e7e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4be11add-d875-5219-b83f-33d16bf37abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-28675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).$\n- The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $\n- $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c8ed87-cb43-5db0-be5c-e831e6121bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295822} {:answer/id #uuid "70fd28f2-8ceb-5fae-b21c-0d8c37373df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295886} {:answer/id #uuid "992814b5-63ad-57d1-9bda-8a6d0aea5b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296088} {:answer/id #uuid "efc85614-0678-5ede-96c5-eeebb1586b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296543} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc665d1-d05c-5d6a-bc20-c9ca8c023c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efc85614-0678-5ede-96c5-eeebb1586b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a661465-5e98-54ef-82ac-034599d77271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b892079-10a1-5d68-a34f-01695fac566e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288066" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $-3u+4=-3u-1$\n- $2u+5=2u+8$\n- $6u-2=3u+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+4 & =-3u-1 \\\\ -3u+4+3u & =-3u-1+3u \\\\ 4 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $-3u+4=-3u-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+5 & =2u+8 \\\\ 2u+5-2u & =2u+8-2u \\\\ 5 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $2u+5=2u+8$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6u-2 & =3u+1 \\\\ 6u-2-3u+2 & =3u+1-3u+2 \\\\ 3u & =3 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $6u-2=3u+1$ has the unique solution $u=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e935809-7eff-5524-8f2e-8f4f21f5d685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295440} {:answer/id #uuid "276266b0-8b3d-5094-8f78-2c2d0df618f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295484} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc10199-d63c-5736-b2b0-1c0ef77650f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295796} {:answer/id #uuid "e00d1820-af62-5599-b6e8-6dccf62f726a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296455} {:answer/id #uuid "ff69cb28-f522-54a1-8193-c2a2130eae30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "276266b0-8b3d-5094-8f78-2c2d0df618f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295484} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f3788ac-8209-560a-917f-1cc30dcfba9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e1fdbd8-4927-548c-aafb-62f0ed007aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-288070" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $-4y+2=-4y-5$\n- $6y+3=6y-1$\n- $5y-7=2y+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2 & =-4y-5 \\\\ -4y+2+4y & =-4y-5+4y \\\\ 2 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $-4y+2=-4y-5$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+3 & =6y-1 \\\\ 6y+3-6y & =6y-1-6y \\\\ 3 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $6y+3=6y-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-7 & =2y+1 \\\\ 5y-7-2y+7 & =2y+1-2y+7 \\\\ 3y & =8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5y-7=2y+1$ has the unique solution $y=\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f682ce-814d-576c-9d68-46eaffd55e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295506} {:answer/id #uuid "34d91129-90ea-5ce1-ba6a-b94c177abdbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295562} {:answer/id #uuid "5d99b17d-1345-5edc-934a-f0e572fd837c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295806} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b35403-5d7a-5721-b22e-eb61699d25cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296474} {:answer/id #uuid "f194dee2-3414-5e50-8807-b8317095ddfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d99b17d-1345-5edc-934a-f0e572fd837c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295806} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c9470b-a03d-5e8c-a6cc-e7fb12154e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22403a37-d9b4-5866-bee2-a28d9c27c3b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-288072" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $2z-4=2z+1$\n- $5z+6=5z+6$\n- $3z+2=z+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-4 & =2z+1 \\\\ 2z-4-2z & =2z+1-2z \\\\ -4 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $2z-4=2z+1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+6 & =5z+6 \\\\ 5z+6-5z & =5z+6-5z \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+2 & =z+4 \\\\ 3z+2-z-2 & =z+4-z-2 \\\\ 2z & =2 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3z+2=z+4$ has the unique solution $z=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ec15b0-9006-57c3-a6ed-6a7ffae1f559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295450} {:answer/id #uuid "483443d7-d949-50ec-a7b8-e99c24e9079f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295680} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0712b9-1553-53a1-83a6-c73d8f9df552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296012} {:answer/id #uuid "8f108ee3-0807-5637-839d-91c354641014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296029} {:answer/id #uuid "9d46e37c-b28f-5c45-9927-cdb6e5e1fe53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f108ee3-0807-5637-839d-91c354641014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296029} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8696e8-0827-512c-9081-1eb13fb3de19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae9bca83-e68f-5756-9519-1447b4343c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-288073" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $3w+2=2w+2$\n- $4w-7=4w-7$\n- $6w+5=6w+9$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w+2 & =2w+2 \\\\ 3w+2-2w-2 & =2w+2-2w-2 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3w+2=2w+2$ has the unique solution $w=0.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w-7 & =4w-7 \\\\ 4w-7-4w & =4w-7-4w \\\\ -7 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $w.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6w+5 & =6w+9 \\\\ 6w+5-6w & =6w+9-6w \\\\ 5 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $w.$ Therefore, the equation $6w+5=6w+9$ has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
